{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["<b>And the children of Israel saw, etc.</b> One needs to know for what matter the verse returned to inform us of this. Perhaps it is to convey to us that Israel needed to see the thing on other occasions to verify that ״the skin of his face was radiant״ — because he was concerned that this light had flashed forth on his face on account of his having detained HaShem, and with the passage of time the thing would return to what it had been. Therefore they would gaze at his face at all times to verify that ״it was radiant,״ etc. Or perhaps it is because the crown returned to its former state in the aspect of his face, after the manner of what they of blessed memory said (Genesis Rabbah 20:12): in Rabbi Meir's scroll it is written ״tunings of light״ (כתנות אור) — so too Moses' facial skin became light (אור)." "<b>Further,</b> it intends that although Moses made the veil, they were not therefore forbidden to look at his face when he was unveiled, but rather <b>and the children of Israel saw the face of Moses</b> — and the matter is understood, for they were not looking in a manner of prohibition, since he did not object to them; and it is the desire of HaShem to gaze in the light of life." "<b>End of parashat Ki Tissa</b>"], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:36:44.421652069Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}